{
 "thread": "x6GiUPIaLa4",
 "subject": "small sample size per stratum code",
 "messages_listed": 3,
 "last_activity": "2019-11-26T20:52:47Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "CLZwCiV8AQAJ",
   "from": "Antonela Marozzi",
   "author": "9ca502218c2e2949",
   "date": "2019-11-25T13:20:54Z",
   "subject": "small sample size per stratum code",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hello dear all, I am estimating densities using Rdist, I have a very simple question. Section 5.3 of Buckland et al. 2015 states that a solution for a small sample size per stratum is to allow the scale parameter of the model to differ among strata. I am not sure how to do this. I would like to know if calling the ds function in the following way is enough or if something else is missing to change the scale parameter.</div><div><br></div><div>hr08_770&lt;-ds (oct08, truncation = 770, key=&quot;hr&quot;, adjustment = NULL, formula = ~size + strata ,convert.units = 0.001 )</div><div>               </div><div>Where strata are the different stratum of my survey. </div><div>Kind regards</div><div>Antonela</div><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "dkeG6JeQBwAJ",
   "from": "Eric Rexstad",
   "author": "f2122b34f1aa6e95",
   "date": "2019-11-25T14:35:55Z",
   "subject": "Re: [distance-sampling] small sample size per stratum code",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "\r\n  \r\n    \r\n  \r\n  <div text=\"#000000\" bgcolor=\"#FFFFFF\">\r\n    <p>Yes Antonela</p>\r\n    <p>You will fit a single detection function with both group size and\r\n      stratum as covariates with the code you have provided.  This is\r\n      assuming you have a field in your data called &quot;strata&quot; and it\r\n      represents the labels of your strata.  More likely, you have used\r\n      &quot;Region.Label&quot; as the field naming your strata; if this is the\r\n      case you would replace &quot;strata&quot; with &quot;as.factor(Region.Label)&quot; in\r\n      your formula.</p>\r\n    <p>Be aware this model might create identifiability problems\r\n      depending whether you are treating group size as a continuous or\r\n      factor covariate.  Factor covariate models are more complex to\r\n      fit, particularly when there are multiple covariates each with\r\n      multiple levels.<br>\r\n    </p></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div text=\"#000000\" bgcolor=\"#FFFFFF\">\r\n    <div>On 25/11/2019 13:20, Antonela Marozzi\r\n      wrote:<br>\r\n    </div>\r\n    </div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div text=\"#000000\" bgcolor=\"#FFFFFF\"><blockquote type=\"cite\">\r\n      \r\n      <div dir=\"ltr\">\r\n        <div>Hello dear all, I am estimating densities using Rdist, I\r\n          have a very simple question. Section 5.3 of Buckland et al.\r\n          2015 states that a solution for a small sample size per\r\n          stratum is to allow the scale parameter of the model to differ\r\n          among strata. I am not sure how to do this. I would like to\r\n          know if calling the ds function in the following way is enough\r\n          or if something else is missing to change the scale parameter.</div>\r\n        <div><br>\r\n        </div>\r\n        <div>hr08_770&lt;-ds (oct08, truncation = 770, key=&quot;hr&quot;,\r\n          adjustment = NULL, formula = ~size + strata ,convert.units =\r\n          0.001 )</div>\r\n        <div>               </div>\r\n        <div>Where strata are the different stratum of my survey. </div>\r\n        <div>Kind regards</div>\r\n        <div>Antonela</div>\r\n        <div><br>\r\n        </div>\r\n      </div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div text=\"#000000\" bgcolor=\"#FFFFFF\"><blockquote type=\"cite\">\r\n      -- <br>\r\n      You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google\r\n      Groups &quot;distance-sampling&quot; group.<br>\r\n      To unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it,\r\n      send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">distance-sampl...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\n      To view this discussion on the web visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/distance-sampling/129f4d64-8a47-41fc-a5d2-2aa8ac8a343d%40googlegroups.com?utm_medium=email&amp;utm_source=footer\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/distance-sampling/129f4d64-8a47-41fc-a5d2-2aa8ac8a343d%2540googlegroups.com?utm_medium%3Demail%26utm_source%3Dfooter&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792473746964000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2904iXaKOpLLZAKElQ84kt\">https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/distance-sampling/129f4d64-8a47-41fc-a5d2-2aa8ac8a343d%40googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\n    </blockquote>\r\n    <pre cols=\"72\">-- \r\nEric Rexstad\r\nResearch Unit for Wildlife Population Assessment\r\nCentre for Research into Ecological and Environmental Modelling\r\nUniversity of St. Andrews\r\nSt. Andrews Scotland KY16 9LZ\r\n<a href=\"tel:+44%201334%20461833\" value=\"+441334461833\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\">+44 (0)1334 461833</a>\r\nThe University of St Andrews is a charity registered in Scotland : No SC013532</pre>\r\n  </div>\r\n\r\n"
    ]
